#!/bin/sh
# Working rule 10 — `main` is only ever advanced by merging a pull request.
#
# **This is the drift catcher, and drift is the recorded failure.** Eleven commits reached `main` on
# 2026-08-18 because a session was still checked out there after an earlier squash-merge and nobody
# looked. The remedy in the rule is "check the branch before the first edit", which is the same control
# that had already failed once — a rule cannot fire an interrupt. This can.
#
# Rule 8's shape: refuse, and be loud about which branch and what to do instead.
#
# **Wired by `git config core.hooksPath hooks`**, which is per-clone and NOT carried by the repository.
# `git config core.hooksPath` printing nothing means this file is inert. `docs/WORKING-RULES.md` rule 10
# says so, because an unstated absence reads as enforcement.
#
# Not a security boundary: `--no-verify` bypasses it and so does unsetting `core.hooksPath`. It is not
# meant to stop a decision, only to stop an accident.
#
# **What it deliberately does NOT catch**, because `pre-commit` never runs for these: a clean `git merge`
# (that is `pre-merge-commit`), and `git cherry-pick`/`git revert` that apply without conflict. A local
# `git merge --squash` followed by `git commit` IS refused, and that is intended rather than overlooked — a
# branch squashed straight onto `main` never passed through a pull request, which is the thing rule 10 is
# about. Use the waiver if you mean it.

branch=$(git symbolic-ref --short -q HEAD) || exit 0   # detached HEAD (rebase, bisect): nothing to guard
[ "$branch" = "main" ] || exit 0

# **An in-progress merge, cherry-pick or revert is not drift, and refusing it is a trap.** A CLEAN merge runs
# `pre-merge-commit`, not this hook — but a CONFLICTED one concludes with a literal `git commit`, which lands
# here. Refusing that is unescapable: `git switch -c` is rejected outright while merging, so the recovery this
# hook prints does not apply and git's own hint is `git merge --quit`, which throws the conflict resolution
# away. Found by review, and it is the prose defect this rule was rewritten to remove ("a prohibition with no
# usable recovery") reintroduced in shell. These three markers are written by git and removed when the
# operation completes or aborts, so they cannot silently linger and disable the guard.
git_dir=$(git rev-parse --git-dir 2>/dev/null) || exit 0
for marker in MERGE_HEAD CHERRY_PICK_HEAD REVERT_HEAD; do
  if [ -e "$git_dir/$marker" ]; then exit 0; fi
done

if [ "$PI_DADDY_ALLOW_MAIN" = "1" ]; then
  echo "pre-commit: PI_DADDY_ALLOW_MAIN=1 set — allowing this commit on main. Working rule 10 waived." >&2
  exit 0
fi

cat >&2 <<'MSG'

  REFUSED: you are on `main`, and `main` is only advanced by merging a pull request.

  This is working rule 10 (docs/WORKING-RULES.md). You have almost certainly drifted here after a
  merge rather than decided to be here — that is exactly how the last eleven commits happened.

  Your work is not lost and nothing needs rewriting. Move it to a branch:

      git switch -c <branch>          # your staged changes come with you
      git commit                      # then push and open a PR

  If this really must land on `main` (and it almost never must):

      PI_DADDY_ALLOW_MAIN=1 git commit ...

MSG
exit 1
